<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakob Böhme</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1575–1624</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jacob Boehme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jakob Böhme was a German Lutheran mystic and philosopher, a shoemaker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>. His first work, Aurora (1612), describing visions of God and nature, led the town's pastor to have him banned from writing. His later writings influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a>, English religious movements, German idealist philosophy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jakob-bohme.jpg" alt="Jakob Böhme" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Gottlob Glymann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacob-B%C3%B6hme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
